{
  "concept": "The tangent line to a curve at a point P is the limiting position of the secant lines through P and a nearby point Q as Q slides along the curve toward P.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "x-axis with arrowhead and label 'x', y-axis with arrowhead and label 'y' (panel a only)",
    "Panel (a): smooth blue hump-shaped curve rising from the left, peaking, then falling to the right",
    "Panel (b): blue circle of moderate radius, with P near the upper-left of the circumference",
    "Fixed blue point P on the curve with italic label 'P'",
    "Movable blue point Q on the curve with italic label 'Q'",
    "Active pink/magenta secant line drawn through P and Q, extended beyond both points",
    "Faded 'ghost' secant lines from previous Q positions, fanning out from P",
    "Pink tangent line at P, drawn as the steepest/limiting line of the fan",
    "Shaded magenta wedge between the tangent line and the current secant line, narrowing as Q approaches P",
    "Curved gray arc with open arrowhead sweeping from the shallow secant direction toward the tangent direction",
    "Text label 'Tangent line' with leader to the limiting line",
    "Text label 'Secant line' with leader to the shallowest secant",
    "Panel captions '(a)' and '(b)'",
    "Live readout box: coordinates of P and Q, secant slope (Δy/Δx), tangent slope, and the gap |Q − P|",
    "Small right-triangle rise/run annotation on the secant showing Δy and Δx"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "qApproach",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "As the horizontal gap between Q and P shrinks toward zero, the secant slope stops changing and settles on a single number — the tangent slope.",
      "label": "Distance of Q from P (Δx)",
      "range": [0.02, 2.5, 0.02],
      "default": 2.0
    },
    {
      "id": "panel",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The same limiting construction defines the tangent on an arbitrary smooth curve and reproduces the familiar 'touches at one point' tangent to a circle.",
      "label": "Curve",
      "options": ["(a) Smooth curve", "(b) Circle"],
      "default": "(a) Smooth curve"
    },
    {
      "id": "movePoint",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "The tangent direction is a property of the point, not of the whole curve — moving P changes the limiting line's slope, and the slope is zero exactly at the curve's peak."
    },
    {
      "id": "showFan",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Seeing all past secants at once makes the rotation visible: the family of secants sweeps monotonically toward one limiting line rather than wandering.",
      "label": "Show secant fan trail",
      "default": true
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A secant through two points",
      "narration": "P is pinned on the curve and Q sits far away. The pink line through them is a secant — it cuts the curve at two points, and its slope is just the rise over run between P and Q.",
      "state": { "panel": "(a) Smooth curve", "qApproach": 2.0, "showFan": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide Q toward P",
      "narration": "Drag Q closer. The secant pivots about P, and the slope readout climbs steadily — each new position of Q gives a different, but progressively less different, line.",
      "state": { "panel": "(a) Smooth curve", "qApproach": 0.9, "showFan": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "The fan closes on one line",
      "narration": "With the trail of earlier secants shown, the rotation is unmistakable: the lines fan out from P and crowd toward a single limiting direction. The shaded wedge between secant and tangent is nearly gone.",
      "state": { "panel": "(a) Smooth curve", "qApproach": 0.25, "showFan": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "The limit: the tangent line",
      "narration": "As Δx → 0 the secant slope stops moving and equals the tangent slope. The tangent is defined as this limiting position — not as 'the line that touches once'.",
      "state": { "panel": "(a) Smooth curve", "qApproach": 0.02, "showFan": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Does it match the circle?",
      "narration": "Switch to the circle with Q far from P: the secant is an ordinary chord, clearly crossing the circle twice.",
      "state": { "panel": "(b) Circle", "qApproach": 2.0, "showFan": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same limit, classical answer",
      "narration": "Let Q slide around to P and the chords rotate into the line perpendicular to the radius at P — exactly the tangent you learned in geometry. The limit definition generalizes it without contradicting it.",
      "state": { "panel": "(b) Circle", "qApproach": 0.02, "showFan": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A tangent line isn't 'the line that touches the curve once' — it's the line the secants rotate into as the second point slides in, which is why the same definition works for a circle, a hump, and any smooth curve.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two-panel layout side by side, white background. Left panel (a): thin black x- and y-axes with arrowheads, labeled 'x' and 'y'; a bold blue (≈#1BA0D7) smooth hump curve entering low on the left, rising through P, cresting, and dropping off to the right. Right panel (b): the same bold blue as a large circle, with P on the upper-left arc. In both panels, P and Q are filled blue dots with italic serif labels. Secant and tangent lines are medium-weight magenta/plum (≈#A64B9E), drawn long so they extend past the curve on both sides; older secants render at reduced opacity to form a visible fan radiating from P. Between the current secant and the tangent, fill a soft magenta gradient wedge that fades outward and visibly narrows as Q approaches P. A thin gray curved arc with a hollow open arrowhead sweeps from the shallow secant region up to the tangent, indicating the direction of rotation. Place 'Tangent line' text in the upper-left near the steepest line and 'Secant line' text at the right near the shallowest line, each with a short leader. Captions '(a)' and '(b)' centered beneath each panel. A small readout panel (neutral gray text) reports P, Q, secant slope, tangent slope, and Δx, with the secant slope visually converging to the tangent slope as the slider approaches zero. Dragging P should relocate the entire fan and wedge with it; at the crest of the hump the tangent should render horizontal with slope 0."
}

